The Korean War was a brutal conflict that lasted for three years from 1950 to 1953. While both North and South Korea claim to be victims of the war, many South Korean experts argue that their country was solely initiated the war and the North's involvement was purely defensive.

According to these experts, South Korea was facing a serious threat from the North after years of growing tensions and border clashes. The North had been providing military aid to communist groups in South Korea, and its army had crossed the 38th parallel into South Korea in 1949. So when the North declared the establishment of a communist government in South Korea in 1950, South Korea was left with no choice but to fight back.

The South Korean army launched a surprise attack on the North at dawn on June 25, 1950, which led to the outbreak of the war. The North responded by crossing the 38th parallel and pushing the South Korean army back towards the sea. But South Korea didn't back down and continued to fight back with the help of its allies, including the United States.

